{"answer":"Let R(x)=(x^2-4)/(x-2). Its domain is R\\{2}, since x-2=0 exactly at x=2. For x≠2, x^2-4=(x-2)(x+2), so R(x)=x+2 by cancellation of the nonzero factor x-2. Thus the simplified expression is x+2, conditional on x≠2. It agrees with the original at every input in the original domain, but they are not equivalent as functions on all real numbers: x+2 is defined at x=2 and equals 4 there, whereas R is undefined there.","approaches":[{"description":"Factor the numerator, identify the denominator’s zero, and cancel only after restricting to inputs where the denominator is nonzero.","id":"conditional-cancellation","outcome":"candidate","reason":"This proves both the simplification and the precise domain comparison."},{"description":"Cancel x-2 without first stating x≠2, then call the result equivalent over all reals.","id":"unconditional-cancellation","outcome":"rejected","reason":"At x=2 the original quotient has zero denominator, so cancellation cannot establish an identity there."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"The question specifies: \"For real x where the expression is defined, simplify (x^2 - 4)/(x - 2).\"","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"given-expression","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Define R(x)=(x^2-4)/(x-2) wherever this quotient is defined.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in denominator-zero and original-domain.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["given-expression"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"original-domain","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The domain of R is all real x except 2.","step_ids":["denominator-zero","original-domain"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in factor-numerator.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"numerator-factorization","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every real x, x^2-4=(x-2)(x+2).","step_ids":["factor-numerator"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in conditional-cancel under the nonzero-denominator condition established in original-domain.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["original-domain","numerator-factorization"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"conditional-simplification","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every real x≠2, R(x)=x+2.","step_ids":["conditional-cancel"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in excluded-input-comparison.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["original-domain","conditional-simplification"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"all-real-comparison","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"R and x+2 agree at every input in the domain of R, but are not equivalent at every real input: x+2 is defined at 2 with value 4, while R is undefined at 2.","step_ids":["excluded-input-comparison"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"denominator-zero","justification":"Solving x-2=0 gives x=2; conversely, substituting x=2 gives 0.","statement":"The denominator x-2 is zero exactly when x=2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["denominator-zero"],"id":"original-domain","justification":"A real quotient is defined exactly when its denominator is nonzero.","statement":"R is defined exactly for real x≠2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"factor-numerator","justification":"Expanding (x-2)(x+2) gives x^2-4.","statement":"x^2-4=(x-2)(x+2) for every real x."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["original-domain","factor-numerator"],"id":"conditional-cancel","justification":"For x≠2, x-2 is nonzero, so ((x-2)(x+2))/(x-2)=x+2.","statement":"For every x in the domain of R, R(x)=x+2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["original-domain","conditional-cancel"],"id":"excluded-input-comparison","justification":"At x=2, x+2=4. By original-domain, R is not defined at 2; conditional-cancel establishes agreement only when x≠2.","statement":"The simplified expression extends the original expression to x=2 rather than being identical to it on all real inputs."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}

